3.text:
In the text as it is normally printed in English the overcoat / coat that Ochumelov is wearing is the little external index of the way he is “changing colour”.  

In the Garnett / standard English text the overcoat is brought up with Ochumelov on entry and then is made to change with him:

* he comes up to the crowd in / with his overcoat on;
* the coat is taken off / unbuttoned as he indulges his momentary self-importance;
* it is put on / buttoned up again as fear makes him retreat.

That is the three substantive moments of coat-description that the criticism habitually points to - an arrival coat, a confident unbuttoned / removed coat and a fearful buttoned-up / re-donned coat. In the ordinary text the word coat/overcoat is referred to four times in order to make those three shifts explicit, and it is those three shifts that are read as the objective correlative of Ochumelov’s chameleonic fear.

